✅ 18. Synchronize on Different Objects

18.1 Definition
🔴 A synchronized block acquires the monitor of the object specified inside synchronized(...).
🔴 If two synchronized blocks use different objects, they use different monitors, so they do not block each other.

18.2 Why Do We Need Multiple Locks?

Imagine a banking application.

You have:
🔴 Customer Database
🔴 Loan Database

Should updating the customer database stop another thread from updating the loan database?
❌ No.

These are completely independent resources.
Instead of one global lock, we create two locks.

Customer Lock
Loan Lock

Now both operations can execute simultaneously.
This improves concurrency significantly.

18.3 Example Without Separate Locks

class Service {
    public synchronized void updateCustomer() {

    }

    public synchronized void updateLoan() {

    }
}

Question:

Can

Thread-A
↓
updateCustomer()

and

Thread-B
↓
updateLoan()

execute simultaneously?
❌ No.

Because both methods synchronize on this Same monitor.

18.4 Better Design

class Service {
    private final Object customerLock = new Object();
    private final Object loanLock = new Object();
}

Now

synchronized(customerLock){

}

and

synchronized(loanLock){

}

are independent.

